python: link-tree links independent of the checkout.

make_py_package_modules linked each module into the link tree relative
to the link's directory, after resolving symlinks on both ends. The
resolved paths depend on the checkout: a source reached through a
symlink into the Nix store (a prelude or toolchain file) resolves
outside the checkout, so its relative link climbs to / and depends on
the checkout's depth; and whether an artifact resolves there depends on
how buck2 materialized it. Two checkouts of the same revision then build
different link trees, and a cached python_test never hits across them.

Compute the link from the paths as given instead. buck2 passes the same
project-relative paths in every checkout, and the links still resolve
through the same symlinks at run time.

--- a/python/tools/make_py_package_modules.py
+++ b/python/tools/make_py_package_modules.py
@@ -251,7 +251,14 @@
     if copy_files:
         link_path = os.path.realpath(src)
     else:
-        link_path = os.path.relpath(_realpath(src), _realpath(new_dest.parent))
+        # Relative to the paths as given, without resolving symlinks: buck2
+        # passes the same project-relative paths in every checkout, so the
+        # link is the same everywhere. Resolving first made it depend on how
+        # an artifact was materialized (a copy, or a symlink into the Nix
+        # store) and on how deep the checkout is.
+        link_path = os.path.relpath(
+            os.path.abspath(src), os.path.abspath(new_dest.parent)
+        )
     if new_dest in path_mapping:
         prev, prev_origin = path_mapping[new_dest]
         if prev != link_path and not (
